title: Turning a script into a brief and an unhappy playbook
description: An algorithm run by hand over a source file, in two steps. The first reads the source once and builds a structure — the steps of the happy path, the functions, the failures. The second writes that structure into a brief and an unhappy playbook.
sections:
  - title: 1 · Read the source into a structure
    body: |
      Nothing is written to a book in this step. Set up the state, then move a cursor over the
      source; what the cursor meets says which section below to take.

      1. Set up the state:
         - `steps` ← an empty list — the happy path, one entry per top-level step.
         - `functions` ← an empty list — one entry per function met.
         - `failures` ← an empty list — one entry per way the run can fail.
         - `step` ← 1 — the number the next top-level step takes.
         - `cursor` ← the first line of the source.
      2. Read what is at `cursor` and take the section for it: a comment, a piece of a
         function, a statement. Do its steps in order, then move `cursor` to the next thing.
      3. Repeat until the end of the source is reached, then take "The end of the source is
         reached".
    children:
      - title: The structure
        body: |
          What this step builds. Every entry describes the file's code and nothing else — not
          how the script is invoked, who runs it, what the caller does with its output, or the
          platform it runs on.

          An entry of `steps`:
          - `number` — 1, 2, 3 …; inside a fork's side, after the fork's number: 9a, 9b.
          - `title` — what the step defines or produces: "Define `mode`", "Load the master".
          - `sentence` — one sentence, in the reader's terms, saying what it does; a paragraph
            where the shape it describes needs one. The argument the script receives, the
            files it reads and what it writes are the file's business; who passes the
            argument, who runs it and who reads the output are not.
          - `children` — optional: one entry per name, when several bindings belong together.
          - `shape` — optional: a fenced block — one item of a loop's result, or the whole
            artifact of an output.
          - `fork` — optional: the question the code asks, one answer per value it compares
            against, and one list of steps per answer.

          An entry of `functions`: the name, the parameters by name, and one sentence a caller
          can use, saying what it does.

          An entry of `failures`:
          - `condition` — in the world's terms, not the code's: "the master playbook is
            missing", not "existsSync returns false".
          - `step` — the number of the top-level step it interrupts; empty until known; a
            function's name or a folded statement's name stands in until then.
          - `then` — what the script does under it: the output it writes, in full, when the
            brief will not show it; how the run ends ("exit 0, nothing after this step runs"),
            or, when the run goes on, which names end up empty and which block of the output
            appears or disappears.
          - `by` — optional: a fork's question, when the behaviour under this failure differs
            by the answer, and `then` per answer.
      - title: A comment is met
        body: |
          A comment can do more than one of these; take each part of it separately.

          1. The part that says what the script never does, or what a broken file yields:
             append what it says to `failures` with no step yet. At the end of the source it
             is merged into the guard's entry that names the same condition.
          2. The part that says how the script is wired or who runs it: record nothing.
          3. The rest is intent: use it as a step's title, as the sentence a step needs, or —
             above a definition — as the function's sentence. Copy no failure word from it.
      - title: A piece of a function is met
        body: |
          A definition or a call. Take the child for the piece the cursor is on. A statement
          that holds a call also raises "A statement is met": do both, the statement's steps
          first.
        children:
          - title: A definition — a function or a closure is defined
            body: |
              1. Add no step: the happy path never shows a definition.
              2. Append to `functions`: the function's name, its parameters by name, and one
                 sentence a caller can use, saying what it does. The comment above the
                 definition is that sentence when there is one, in the reader's terms — "the
                 one entry of a version-2 event, each entry of a version-1 event's list", not
                 "the list as it is, or the mapping as a one-item list".
              3. For each guard, catch or early exit in its body, and for each shape it
                 assumes with no guard: append to `failures` its condition and its `then`,
                 with the function's name in place of a step.
              4. Move `cursor` past the definition. Its body is not walked as steps.
          - title: A call — a function is called
            body: |
              1. Find the function in `functions`.
              2. In the sentence of the step the call belongs to, write what the function does
                 at this place, with the arguments it was given, in the reader's terms: not
                 "define `doc` to be `load` of `MASTER`" but "read the file at `MASTER` as
                 text and define `doc` to be that text parsed as one YAML document".
              3. Write it in full at every call, never "as in step 4". Duplication is what
                 makes each step readable on its own.
              4. For each entry of `failures` marked with this function's name: add this
                 step's number to it. A function called at two steps fails at both.
      - title: A statement is met
        body: |
          What the statement does decides what is recorded. Test the children top to bottom,
          take the first that holds, and do its steps in order: a guard before a fork, a fork
          before a loop, a loop before an output, an output before a binding, a binding before
          language-only. A require that binds a name a later step uses is a binding. A
          conditional expression inside a binding is not a fork; it is the binding's sentence.

          Whichever child is taken, then also:

          1. If the statement does something to a value that crashes when the value is not
             the shape assumed — a list mapped over, a method called on it — with no guard:
             append to `failures` that shape not holding, with this step's number, and as
             `then` "the run crashes: exit 1, nothing on stdout, the error on stderr". One
             entry per step, naming every shape that crashes it; not one per shape. A key
             read off a mapping that is not there does not crash — it is an empty value
             inside a line, whether the code writes a fallback for it or lets it print as
             nothing, and an empty value inside a line is never an entry.
          2. If the statement holds a call, take "A call — a function is called" as well.
          3. A binding whose value is a function or a closure is a definition: take "A piece
             of a function is met" and not the binding child.
        children:
          - title: It guards, catches or exits early — one side of it is a failure
            body: |
              1. Take the side where nothing is wrong. It is the guard's own block when it
                 has one; for a guard that only exits or throws, it is the one statement the
                 guard protects — the next one. Move `cursor` to its first statement; the
                 statements there raise their own sections and share this step — a guard met
                 inside that side shares it too, and adds its own entry to `failures`. The
                 guard adds no step and does not move `step`; the statements on its side do,
                 and what follows the side is its own step.
              2. Begin that step's sentence with the check when there is one, and write the
                 rest as if it always succeeds: "Check that a file exists at `MASTER`, then
                 define `doc` to be …". A try has no check: write what it tries as if it
                 always succeeds.
              3. Record nothing of the other side in `steps`: no "throws", no "cannot", no
                 note.
              4. Read the other side and append to `failures`: the condition in the world's
                 terms, this step's number, and `then` — the output it writes in full, how the
                 run ends, or, when the run goes on, which names end up empty. A catch that
                 covers two conditions the script cannot tell apart is one entry naming both.
          - title: It branches where neither side is a failure
            body: |
              1. Append one step, numbered `step`, whose sentence says only that what runs next
                 follows the answer: "What runs last follows `mode`."
              2. Give it a `fork`: the question the code asks, one answer per value the code
                 compares against, and one more for the other side when the code has one —
                 "`session`, anything else, or nothing". If a fork on the same question was
                 already recorded, reuse its question and answers.
              3. For each answer: set `cursor` to the first statement of its side and run this
                 step's sections over the side into the answer's own list of steps, numbering
                 them after the step — 9a, 9b.
              4. While walking a side, if an entry of `failures` behaves differently under this
                 answer than under another — the run crashes here and survives there, or takes
                 another branch — set its `by` to this fork's question and record `then` per
                 answer. That the outputs differ is no reason, and a block written under one
                 answer and not the other is an output difference: one `then`, saying so.
              5. Move `cursor` past the fork. `step` ← `step` + 1.
          - title: It loops over a collection
            body: |
              1. Append one step, numbered `step`, for the whole collection; its sentence
                 describes the result and not the iteration: "Define `lines` to be one line
                 per event of `working`: …".
              2. Set its `shape` to a fenced block showing one item.
              3. For each condition inside the loop: if it skips an item, write it into the
                 sentence as part of which items count; if it fails the run, append it to
                 `failures` with this step's number and its `then`.
              4. If the collection can be empty rather than wrong — a list that is missing, a
                 file with no entries — and the name it reads is not already in `failures` as
                 possibly empty: append that to `failures` with this step's number, and as
                 `then` which block of the output appears or disappears, or how the run ends
                 differently. A fallback that replaces a whole line or block — `- (no child
                 book carries a decision)` — is such a block. A fallback inside a line — `(no
                 content yet)`, `(untitled)`, a key standing in for a label — is no failure;
                 it goes into the output's sentence.
              5. Move `cursor` past the loop; its body is not walked as steps. `step` ← `step`
                 + 1.
          - title: It produces an output — a write to stdout, a file written, a value returned to the caller
            body: |
              1. Append one step, numbered `step`, and set its `shape` to the artifact in full
                 as it leaves the script, in a fenced block, with every variable the happy path
                 fills as a placeholder in angle brackets — `<MASTER>`, `<event labels>`.
              2. Write its sentence as one sentence per placeholder saying how it is filled. If
                 a line has a fallback — `(no content yet)`, a key standing in for a label —
                 write it into that sentence.
              3. If the artifact wraps a multi-line text in one serialised string: show the
                 wrapper with one placeholder for the text, then the text with its line breaks
                 written out, and say that is what was done. The reader sees what comes out,
                 not the code that assembles it.
              4. `step` ← `step` + 1.
          - title: It binds a name a later step uses
            body: |
              1. If the name is empty on the happy path and only a failure fills it: add no
                 step and stop. The failure that fills it says where it lands, in its `then`.
              2. Append one step, numbered `step`, titled by the name: "Define `name`".
              3. Write its sentence saying what the name holds, in intent: "Define `mode` to be
                 the argument the script was run with, `session` when there is none". A
                 conditional expression inside the binding goes into that sentence —
                 "`UserPromptSubmit` when `mode` is `prompt`, `SessionStart` otherwise" — and
                 opens no fork.
              4. While the statements that follow bind names that belong with this one: take
                 each into this step as one child per name, and move `cursor` past it. The
                 step is then titled by all the names — "Define `DIR`, `MASTER` and `MEMORY`"
                 — and its sentence moves into the children. A name defined on the happy path
                 that only a failure reads still gets its step.
              5. If the value can be empty rather than wrong: append that to `failures` with
                 this step's number, and as `then` which block of the output appears or
                 disappears, or how the run ends differently; a fallback that replaces a whole
                 line or block counts, a fallback inside a line does not. A later step that
                 reads this name adds no second entry.
              6. For each entry of `failures` marked with this name: set its step to this
                 step's number.
              7. `step` ← `step` + 1.
          - title: It only serves the language and binds no name a later step uses — an import, a require, a coercion, a path joined or an argument read that only the language needs
            body: |
              1. Add no step. The same expression binding a name a later step uses — `MASTER`
                 from a path joined, `mode` from an argument read — is a binding, not this.
              2. Note what the statement provides, so the step that uses it can say it in the
                 reader's terms: not "join `DIR` with the file name" but "define `MASTER` to be
                 `claude.playbook` in `DIR`".
              3. If it can fail: append to `failures` the condition, marked with the name it
                 provides in place of a step, and as `then` what the rest of the run does
                 without the thing — which names end up empty, and what the output looks like
                 as a result. The step that uses the name fills the number in.
      - title: The end of the source is reached
        body: |
          1. Merge `failures`: two entries naming the same condition at the same step are
             one — a guard's own entry and the same condition carried in from a function's
             body, say. Two entries the script's output cannot tell apart — an empty file at
             step 5 and a file with no `events` list at step 7 — are one, named by the wider
             condition and anchored at the step where the script first differs. An entry that came from a comment merges into every entry naming its
             condition, keeping the guard's step and `then` and any wording of the comment
             that says it better; a comment the code contradicts is dropped, and said so in
             the report. A comment entry that matches nothing gets the step that uses what it
             guards.
          2. For each entry of `failures` with no step number: set it to the step that uses
             what the entry guards.
          3. For each entry of `steps`: check that its sentence names what it defines or
             produces, that it depends on no function `functions` does not explain, and that
             nothing in it is about the world outside the file. Rewrite any that fails.
          4. Search every sentence in `steps` for "throws", "cannot", "missing", "error",
             "fails", "catch". For each hit: rewrite the sentence without it, and append what
             it said to `failures` with that step's number.
          5. Take "Write the structure into the two books".
  - title: 2 · Write the structure into the two books
    body: |
      1. Make two files beside each other, from the templates:

         ```
         studio-check --template brief > <name>.brief
         studio-check --template playbook > <name>-unhappy.playbook
         ```

      2. Set the brief's title to what the script is, and its description to say it is the
         happy path and name the unhappy playbook. Set the playbook's description to say each
         event is something that can go wrong at a step of the brief.
      3. Write `steps` into the brief — "The happy-path brief".
      4. Write `failures` into the playbook — "The unhappy playbook".
      5. Take "Check both books".
    children:
      - title: The happy-path brief
        body: |
          1. For each entry of `steps`, in order, append one top-level section: title
             "`number` · `title`", body the sentence. A section is a title and a body — no
             description, and no sentence that restates the structure ("three names, one child
             each").
          2. If the entry has `children`: write one child section per name, title "Define
             `name`", body its sentence — and no body on the parent section, which is its
             title alone.
          3. If the entry has a `shape`: write the fenced block into the body after the
             sentence, and for an output the placeholder sentences after the block.
          4. If the entry has a `fork`: give the section a `content` of `kind: playbook` — one
             decision, the fork's question, with its answers as values; one event per answer,
             labelled by the answer ("The mode is prompt"), `when` that answer, whose content
             is `kind: brief` holding that answer's steps written by these same rules,
             numbered 9a, 9b. Two forks on the same question share one decision.
          5. No word of failure goes in: nothing from `failures` is written here.
      - title: The unhappy playbook
        body: |
          1. For each entry of `failures`, append one event. Its label is the condition, in
             the world's terms. Its `detail` is the top-level step it interrupts: "Step 4."; a
             child of that step is named in the document's text, not in `detail`.
          2. Write its document from `then`, `kind: md`, as numbered steps in the same voice
             as the brief, ending with how the run ends: "Exit 0. Nothing after step 4 runs."
             Where `then` holds an output the brief does not show, show it in full, as an
             output step would.
          3. Where the run goes on, say which names end up empty and which block of the
             output goes. When the output is the same as for another event, say so instead of
             repeating it: "the same text written as for a missing memory".
          4. If the entry has `by`: add to the playbook the same decision as the brief's fork,
             with the same answers, unless it is already there, and write the event's content
             `by` that decision, one document per answer. Otherwise one document, naming the
             effect on the names the brief defines; the brief's fork shows the outputs.
      - title: Check both books
        body: |
          1. Read the brief alone, top to bottom, as a stranger: every step names what it
             defines or produces, no step depends on a function the brief never explains,
             nothing in it is about the world outside the file. Search it for "throws",
             "cannot", "missing", "error", "fails", "catch"; a hit is a sentence to rewrite.
          2. Check that every event's `detail` in the playbook names a top-level step that
             exists in the brief, and that every entry of `failures` became an event.
          3. `studio-check` both files; fix until they pass.
          4. If the script can be run: for each event, build one fixture — a folder of its own
             outside the script's folder, holding a copy of the script with what the event
             needs arranged around it: the file missing, a file that does not parse, a book
             with no list. Nothing is placed in the script's own folder. Run the copy once per
             fixture and the script once on the happy path. Compare what each writes with what
             the books say, and fix the books where they differ.
          5. Delete the structure and the fixtures.
